                    <h1 class="content-title">Entity Framework & <span class="gradient-text">Data Model</span></h1>
                    <p class="content-subtitle">
                        The data model is the heart of IRM. Understanding entities, their types, and how 
                        they connect to risks, controls, and policies through M2M relationships is essential 
                        for any IRM developer.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <!-- Entity Framework -->
                <section class="content-section" id="entity-framework">
                    <h2>Entity Framework</h2>
                    <p>
                        The <strong>Entity Framework</strong> is the foundation of IRM. An "entity" represents any object 
                        in your organization that needs to be monitored for risk and compliance — this could be a server, 
                        a department, a business process, an application, or a vendor.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Entities are formally called <strong>Profiles</strong> in the data model (stored in the 
                        <code>sn_grc_profile</code> table). They are organized using:
                    </p>

                    <div class="accordion">
                        <div class="accordion-item open">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Entity Types (sn_grc_profile_type)</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Entity Types group entities with common characteristics. For example, you might have 
                                        an Entity Type called "Critical Windows Servers" that dynamically includes all Windows 
                                        servers tagged as critical in the CMDB.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        Entity Types can use <strong>filters</strong> to automatically pull in CMDB records. 
                                        When a new CI matches the filter criteria, a corresponding entity is automatically created. 
                                        This is how IRM scales — you don't manually create entities for every asset.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Entity Classes (sn_grc_profile_class)</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Entity Classes provide an additional layer of categorization and hierarchy management. 
                                        They help organize Entity Types into meaningful tiers for reporting and risk aggregation. 
                                        For example, classes like "Tier 1 — Mission Critical" and "Tier 2 — Business Important" 
                                        let you prioritize monitoring.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <div class="accordion-item">
                            <button class="accordion-trigger">
                                <span class="accordion-trigger-text">Content References (sn_grc_content_reference)</span>
                                <span class="accordion-icon">▾</span>
                            </button>
                            <div class="accordion-body">
                                <div class="accordion-content">
                                    <p>
                                        Content References are virtual objects that map requirements across different compliance 
                                        frameworks. They act as the "glue" between authority documents, controls, and entities. 
                                        When a content reference is associated with both a control and an entity, the system 
                                        generates the necessary compliance records for that combination.
                                    </p>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Architecture Insight</div>
                        <p>
                            The relationship is: <strong>Entity Types</strong> define <em>what</em> gets monitored, 
                            <strong>Entity Classes</strong> define <em>how important</em> it is, and 
                            <strong>Content References</strong> define <em>what requirements</em> apply to it.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Core Tables -->
                <section class="content-section" id="core-tables">
                    <h2>Core GRC / IRM Tables</h2>
                    <p>
                        Below are the primary tables you'll work with as an IRM developer. All are in the 
                        <code>sn_grc</code> or <code>sn_compliance</code> application scope.
                    </p>

                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Concept</th>
                                    <th>Table Name</th>
                                    <th>Description</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Entity (Profile)</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_profile</code></td>
                                    <td>Objects being monitored for risk/compliance (servers, depts, processes)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Entity Type</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_profile_type</code></td>
                                    <td>Groups entities by common characteristics with dynamic filters</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Entity Class</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_profile_class</code></td>
                                    <td>Categorization tiers for entity prioritization</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Risk</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_risk_risk</code></td>
                                    <td>Individual risk records with lifecycle state management</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Risk Framework</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_risk_framework</code></td>
                                    <td>Risk categories/frameworks for organizing the risk register</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Authority Document</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_authority_document</code></td>
                                    <td>External regulations and standards (NIST, ISO, GDPR)</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Policy</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_policy</code></td>
                                    <td>Internal organizational policies</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Control</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_control</code></td>
                                    <td>Specific implementations of policy/control objectives</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Control Objective</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_policy_stmt</code></td>
                                    <td>Policy statements / control objectives linking policy → controls</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Indicator</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator</code></td>
                                    <td>Configuration of automated/manual control monitoring tests</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Indicator Result</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_indicator_result</code></td>
                                    <td>Actual Pass/Fail results from indicator runs</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Issue</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_issue</code></td>
                                    <td>Compliance gaps or failed controls that need remediation</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Citation</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_citation</code></td>
                                    <td>References within authority documents or policies</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><strong>Content Reference</strong></td>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_content_reference</code></td>
                                    <td>Virtual mapping objects for cross-framework requirements</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- M2M Relationships -->
                <section class="content-section" id="m2m-relationships">
                    <h2>M2M (Many-to-Many) Relationships</h2>
                    <p>
                        IRM is a highly relational application. Most core objects are connected through 
                        <strong>Many-to-Many (M2M) join tables</strong>. Understanding these is crucial — 
                        they determine how data flows between modules.
                    </p>

                    <div class="data-table-wrapper">
                        <table class="data-table">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>M2M Table</th>
                                    <th>Links</th>
                                    <th>Purpose</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_m2m_cont_ref_control</code></td>
                                    <td>Content Reference ↔ Control</td>
                                    <td>Maps framework requirements to specific controls</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_m2m_cont_ref_profile</code></td>
                                    <td>Content Reference ↔ Entity</td>
                                    <td>Associates requirements with monitored entities</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_grc_m2m_cont_ref_auth_doc</code></td>
                                    <td>Content Reference ↔ Authority Doc</td>
                                    <td>Links requirements back to regulatory sources</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_m2m_citation_control</code></td>
                                    <td>Citation ↔ Control</td>
                                    <td>Maps specific regulation citations to controls</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>sn_compliance_control_objective_item</code></td>
                                    <td>Control Objective ↔ CMDB CI</td>
                                    <td>Links policy objectives to infrastructure items</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Best Practice</div>
                        <p>
                            Avoid writing directly to M2M tables via scripts or integrations. Use the ServiceNow UI 
                            or established APIs instead. The platform has built-in automation (triggers, business rules) 
                            tied to these M2M records that won't fire if you bypass the standard mechanisms.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

