                <!-- CMDB Integration -->
                <section class="content-section" id="cmdb-integration">
                    <h2>CMDB Integration</h2>
                    <p>
                        IRM doesn't duplicate IT asset data — it references the <strong>CMDB</strong> (Configuration 
                        Management Database). The <code>applies_to</code> field on <code>sn_grc_profile</code> is a 
                        reference to <code>cmdb_ci</code>, creating a direct link between risk management entities 
                        and your IT infrastructure.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Common Service Data Model (CSDM)</h3>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow strongly recommends aligning your entity model with the 
                        <strong>CSDM (Common Service Data Model)</strong>. CSDM standardizes how services, 
                        applications, and infrastructure are represented in the CMDB. When your entities 
                        follow CSDM, risk data automatically aligns with your service architecture — making 
                        it possible to answer questions like "What is the total risk exposure of Service X?"
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Developer Tip</div>
                        <p>
                            Use the Entity Type's <strong>Source table</strong> and <strong>Filter</strong> fields 
                            to dynamically populate entities from the CMDB. Set the source table to a CMDB class 
                            (e.g., <code>cmdb_ci_server</code>) and define filter conditions. IRM will automatically 
                            create and maintain entity records for matching CIs.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Exploring Schema -->
                <section class="content-section" id="exploring-schema">
                    <h2>Exploring the Schema</h2>
                    <p>
                        As a developer, you should use the platform's native tools to explore the IRM schema in your instance:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Schema Map:</strong> Navigate to <code>System Definition → Tables</code>, select any 
                            IRM table (e.g., <code>sn_grc_profile</code>), and click <strong>"Show Schema Map"</strong> 
                            in the Related Links. This visually displays all related tables, extensions, and references.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Table Explorer:</strong> Filter the Tables module by the <code>sn_grc</code> prefix to 
                            see all GRC/IRM tables. Check the <code>sn_compliance</code> and <code>sn_risk</code> 
                            prefixes as well.
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <strong>Dictionary:</strong> Use <code>System Definition → Dictionary</code> to explore 
                            individual field definitions, types, and reference targets.
                        </li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="code-block">
                        <div class="code-header">
                            <span class="code-lang">Quick Query — List all IRM tables</span>
                            <button class="code-copy">Copy</button>
                        </div>
                        <div class="code-body">
<pre><code>// Background Script: Find all GRC/IRM tables
var gr = new GlideRecord('sys_db_object');
gr.addQuery('name', 'STARTSWITH', 'sn_grc');
gr.orderBy('name');
gr.query();
while (gr.next()) {
    gs.info(gr.name + ' — ' + gr.label);
}</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

